false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"/2wZ"}]},{"client_msg_id":"31c34acb-fa38-4089-9a80-71c1ff40ca06","type":"message","user":"U03C6TEAZ","text":"apt で file not found とか出てる。","ts":"1626165623.056900","team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"j0HW"}]},{"client_msg_id":"0f85dae6-0cf5-4826-a657-64f2f888c033","type":"message","user":"U06BRP8VD","text":"で、ubuntuのマトリックスがとまったからWindowsもキャンセルされたように見える。","ts":"1626165600.056600","team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"=C/"}]},{"client_msg_id":"f19c200e-6114-4dd8-8c5f-0730652614bd","type":"message","user":"U06BRP8VD","text":"よくわかってないけど、単にubuntuのレポジトリが落ちてたりしない?","ts":"1626165535.056100","team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"=8fe6"}]},{"client_msg_id":"bdcf0eb2-f8ec-4f4e-9d75-eb4ac5f7ffba","type":"message","user":"U03C6TEAZ","text":"\u003chttps://github.com/gonum/hdf5/pull/83\u003e","ts":"1626165329.055600","attachments":[{"color":"36a64f","fallback":"\u003chttps://github.com/gonum/hdf5/pull/83|#83 Fix build on Windows\u003e","id":1,"title":"\u003chttps://github.com/gonum/hdf5/pull/83|#83 Fix build on Windows\u003e","text":"First argument of malloc is size_t. So built on Windows always fail.","fields":[{"title":"Comments","value":"3","short":true}],"mrkdwn_in":["text"],"blocks":null,"footer":"\u003chttps://github.com/gonum/hdf5|gonum/hdf5\u003e","footer_icon":"https://slack.github.com/static/img/favicon-neutral.png","ts":1626161571}],"team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"yl0"}]},{"client_msg_id":"579b6a35-df46-42b4-a79c-01e9522342ec","type":"message","user":"U03C6TEAZ","text":"GitHub Actions 詳しいマンだれか教えてくれー。","ts":"1626165327.055400","team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"8coM"}]},{"client_msg_id":"c139dbfd-ce2b-4d9a-aac2-c6eb502e54f2","type":"message","user":"UERCTTT8B","text":"race が起きたときにプログラムを止めることがある、ということを明言するようにした感じですかね","ts":"1626162603.055000","team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"mLS"}]},{"client_msg_id":"a2d8debc-e597-49ee-9399-dfb645dbd272","type":"message","user":"UERCTTT8B","text":"\u0026gt; Other programming languages typically take one of two approaches to programs containing data races. The first, exemplified by C and C++, is that programs with data races are invalid: a compiler may break them in arbitrarily surprising ways. The second, exemplified by Java and JavaScript, is that programs with data races have defined semantics, limiting the possible impact of a race and making programs more reliable and easier to debug. Go's approach sits between these two. Programs with data races are invalid in the sense that an implementation may report the race and terminate the program. But otherwise, programs with data races have defined semantics with a limited number of outcomes, making errant programs more reliable and easier to debug.\n","ts":"1626162202.054600","team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"umi4"}]},{"client_msg_id":"221dfa30-85a7-42e0-a31b-1c748f7732dd","type":"message","user":"UAZ33BKV2","text":"\u003chttps://uchan.hateblo.jp/entry/2020/06/19/185152\u003e\nもうちょっと読みやすそうなの?","ts":"1626160008.054300","attachments":[{"fallback":"uchan note: データ競合と happens-before 関係 - uchan note","id":1,"title":"データ競合と happens-before 関係 - uchan note","title_link":"https://uchan.hateblo.jp/entry/2020/06/19/185152","text":"この記事は，マルチスレッドのプログラムでしばしば見る，そして発見が困難であるバグ「データ競合」と，それを見つけるのによく使われる「happens-before 関係」について解説します。 筆者は特に x86-64 アーキテクチャにおけるバグ検出に興味がありますので，一般的な話というよりは x86-64 に寄せた話になります。 想定読者 データ競合バグの発生原理やアトミック変数を使ったバグの防止方法を知りたいと思っているプログラマ データ競合バグを検出する手法，特に happens-before 関係を勉強したいプログラム解析手法の研究者 x86-64 \u0026amp; C++ を例に説明しますが，その他のア…","image_url":"https://ogimage.blog.st-hatena.com/6653586347149404892/26006613583281802/1594876563","blocks":null,"ts":1592560312,"service_name":"uchan note","service_icon":"https://uchan.hateblo.jp/icon/touch","from_url":"https://uchan.hateblo.jp/entry/2020/06/19/185152","original_url":"https://uchan.hateblo.jp/entry/2020/06/19/185152"}],"team":"T03C4RC8V","reactions":[{"name":"eyes","count":1,"users":["UEACRCY0Z"]}],"replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"DWs"}]},{"client_msg_id":"81888e20-9e86-4e6d-83e4-2ce9f1d58629","type":"message","user":"UERCTTT8B","text":"それかもしれないです、ありがとうございます","ts":"1626159904.054000","team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"O7a"}]},{"client_msg_id":"fa89eb14-1a19-4eb6-9576-0e971df25106","type":"message","user":"UEACRCY0Z","text":"これかな、と思ったんですけど、違うか\n\u003chttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happened-before\u003e","ts":"1626159756.053600","attachments":[{"fallback":"wikipedia: Happened-before","id":1,"author_name":"Wikipedia","author_link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","title":"Happened-before","title_link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happened-before","text":"In computer science, the happened-before relation (denoted: \n  \n    \n      \n        →\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\to \\;}\n  ) is a relation between the result of two events, such that if one event should happen before another event, the result must reflect that, even if those events are in reality executed out of order (usually to optimize program flow). This involves ordering events based on the potential causal relationship of pairs of events in a concurrent system, especially asynchronous distributed systems. It was formulated by Leslie Lamport.The happened-before relation is formally defined as the least strict partial order on events such that:\n\nIf events \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\;}\n   and \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b\\;}\n   occur on the same process, \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        →\n        b\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\to b\\;}\n   if the occurrence of event \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\;}\n   preceded the occurrence of event \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b\\;}\n  .\nIf event \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\;}\n   is the sending of a message and event \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b\\;}\n   is the reception of the message sent in event \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\;}\n  , \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        →\n        b\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\to b\\;}\n  .If two events happen in different isolated processes (that do not exchange messages directly or indirectly via third-party processes), then the two processes are said to be concurrent, that is neither \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        →\n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\to b}\n   nor \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n        →\n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b\\to a}\n   is true.If there are other causal relationships between events in a given system, such as between the creation of a process and its first event, these relationships are also added to the definition.\nFor example, in some programming languages such as Java, C++ or Rust, a happens-before edge exists if memory written to by statement A is visible to statement B, that is, if statement A completes its write before statement B starts its read.\nLike all strict partial orders, the happened-before relation is transitive, irreflexive and antisymmetric, i.e.:\n\n  \n    \n      \n        ∀\n        a\n        ,\n        b\n        ,\n        c\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\forall a,b,c}\n  , if \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        →\n        b\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\to b\\;}\n   and \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n        →\n        c\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b\\to c\\;}\n  , then \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        →\n        c\n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\to c\\;}\n   (transitivity). This means that for any three events \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        ,\n        b\n        ,\n        c\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a,b,c}\n  , if \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a}\n   happened before \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b}\n  , and \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b}\n   happened before \n  \n    \n      \n        c\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle c}\n  , then \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a}\n   must have happened before \n  \n    \n      \n        c\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle c}\n  .\n\n  \n    \n      \n        ∀\n        a\n        ,\n        a\n        ↛\n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\forall a,a\\nrightarrow a}\n   (irreflexivity). This means that no event can happen before itself.\n\n  \n    \n      \n        ∀\n        a\n        ,\n        b\n        ,\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\forall a,b,}\n   where \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        ≠\n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\neq b}\n  , if \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        →\n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a\\to b}\n   then \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n        ↛\n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b\\nrightarrow a}\n   (antisymmetry). This means that for any two distinct events \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n        ,\n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a,b}\n  , if \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a}\n   happened before \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b}\n   then \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b}\n   cannot have happened before \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a}\n  .The processes that make up a distributed system have no knowledge of the happened-before relation unless they use a logical clock, like a Lamport clock or a vector clock. This allows one to design algorithms for mutual exclusion, and tasks like debugging or optimising distributed systems.","blocks":null,"service_icon":"https://a.slack-edge.com/80588/img/unfurl_icons/wikipedia.png","from_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happened-before","original_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happened-before"}],"edited":{"user":"UEACRCY0Z","ts":"1626159780.000000"},"team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"hdd"}]},{"client_msg_id":"63d9ee3d-ff50-42c3-a885-49f4aa4d0e19","type":"message","user":"UERCTTT8B","text":"happens-before ってどういう意味だろう","ts":"1626159506.053000","thread_ts":"1626102221.049300","subtype":"thread_broadcast","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"Ml5T"}],"root":{"client_msg_id":"4fc1293c-ca2d-4f43-b8c5-5d537859357a","type":"message","user":"U03C6TEAZ","text":"\u003chttps://github.com/golang/go/discussions/47141\u003e","ts":"1626102221.049300","thread_ts":"1626102221.049300","reply_count":1,"team":"T03C4RC8V","replace_original":false,"delete_original":false,"blocks":[{"type":"rich_text","block_id":"z=N9f"}]}},{"client_msg_id":"e61d538a-14fb-423e-910d-0e76029f78cc","type":"message","user":"UERCTTT8B","text":"\u003chttps://twitter.com/hnakamur2/status/1414838364629471281\u003e まじだ","ts":"1626158983.052800","attachments":[{"fallback":"\u003chttps://twitter.com/hnakamur2|@hnakamur2\u003e: そして \u003chttps://golang.org/\u003e の Packages のリンク\n\u003chttps://golang.org/pk